# Explicitly authored reading decisions, November 19 2026.
item(1,'reading question',[2,3],'3–34','topolog. fppf; Regardons d’abord','In characteristic p>0 the page distinguishes cohomological dimension for all p-primary sheaves on the finite faithfully flat site from the supremum obtained using finite commutative p-power group schemes. The latter is denoted cd′p.','The inclusion of representable sheaves gives one inequality; equality and a comparison with the full fppf site are not written. The finite-flat topology must not silently be replaced by the modern big fppf site.','agrees','The note accurately separates two definitions; neither the cover exponent nor equality of dimensions is settled by the text.')
item(2,'unproved claim',[3],'18–34','G′⊂G″⊂G; G″=G⁰','For a finite commutative p-power group scheme over k of characteristic p, a connected–étale and unipotent–multiplicative filtration is asserted. Vanishing in degrees >n is reduced to étale, radicial unipotent and radicial multiplicative constituents.','The short exact sequences are reconstructed in the category of abelian flat sheaves; existence of the particular subgroup G′ is asserted, not constructed.','agrees','The note requests exactly the missing construction and preserves the ground-field issue.')
item(3,'withdrawn in the same text',[5],'72–74','égal à 0 ou 1; toute [deleted]','The text assigns étale p-cohomological dimension 0 when finite separable extensions have degree prime to p, and 1 otherwise, but cancels its universal quantifier.','The mathematically precise test is: every finite separable extension has prime-to-p degree, equivalently the absolute Galois group is pro-prime-to-p. The surviving singular wording alone does not supply that quantifier.','agrees','Cancellation of toute and insertion of finie are present; a rigorous formulation is reconstruction, not an ink correction.')
item(4,'unproved claim',[5,7],'76–102;136–146','nul pour i>n, et p-divisible si i=n','The finite multiplicative p-group dimension is ≤n exactly when, for every k-torus T, the p-primary component of H^i(k,T) vanishes for i>n and H^n(k,T) is p-divisible. The displayed [p] exact sequence gives kernel and cokernel terms in adjacent degrees.','Cohomology is flat/Galois for smooth tori; the exact sequence gives 0→H^i(T)/p→H^{i+1}(T[p])→H^{i+1}(T)[p]→0. This corrects any ambiguous subscript convention only in the explanatory statement.','agrees','The two adjacent degree terms are on scan7; sufficiency requires more than simply naming p-divisible groups.')
item('5,8','unproved claim',[5,7,9],'93–94;156–170;205–223','il suffit d’ordre premier à p; se dévisse … à μp','To prove the torus criterion one passes to a finite separable extension of degree prime to p over which the remaining splitting group is a p-group. A finite p-primary multiplicative group then has a filtration with μp quotients; its Cartier-dual module has a composition series with trivial Fp constituents. A norm from restriction of scalars and dimension shifting give the stated surjection in H^{n+1}.','Selection of a p-Sylow splitting subgroup, Cartier duality, and the norm are the reconstructed mechanisms. Neither an arbitrary splitting extension nor an arbitrary finite multiplicative group has the asserted μp filtration over the original field.','agrees','The notes preserve the prime-to-p reduction and identify a real omitted proof detail, not a new conjecture.')
item(6,'withdrawn in the same text',[7,9],'143–154;205–223','reste à vérifier; C’est un hom. de gr. p-divisibles [deleted]','The unfinished argument for surjectivity H^n(T)→H^n(T′) is struck. The replacement is the prime-to-p norm and μp filtration on scan9.','No assertion that a homomorphism between divisible groups is automatically surjective is retained.','agrees','Both the cancelled argument and the subsequent replacement occur; the proof is abbreviated but not simply abandoned.')
item(7,'reading question',[7],'156–161','H^i(k,∏k′/k G′)≃H^i(k,G)','The written restriction-of-scalars identity has a right-hand coefficient G over k that the sentence does not introduce. The expected Shapiro identity is H^i(k,Res(k′/k)G′)=H^i(k′,G′), for a finite separable field extension and an abelian coefficient sheaf/group.','Right-hand k′ and G′ are mathematical reading candidates, not a transcription replacement.','agrees','The domain mismatch is visible in the full line and is not resolved by the note itself.')
item(9,'unproved claim',[9],'225–231','un groupe radiciel additif se dévisse en groupes αp','For finite radicial unipotent commutative group schemes in characteristic p, the page claims dimension 0 over a perfect field and 1 over an imperfect field. For αp, H^1=k/k^p and H^i=0 for i>1 by the Frobenius sequence and additive acyclicity.','The αp calculation is reconstructed. A filtration over the stated field, rather than merely after scalar extension, is a separate needed structural input.','agrees','The page stops after the dévissage; the note does not claim it supplies the omitted calculation.')
item('10,11','unproved claim',[4,6],'55–67;107–122','Homomorphismes centraux; u_j sera central pour j grand','Let H be a finite-type multiplicative-type group and G a finitely presented group scheme over S. If u,v:H→G agree on a fiber and u is central, they agree on a neighborhood. The proof uses formal rigidity, density of finite torsion, and descent to finite-type Z-subalgebras.','The published theorem weakens centrality to the special fiber. Finite-stage descent of centrality follows by descending equality of the commutator and unit morphisms between finitely presented schemes; the source gives the assertion, not these equations.','agrees','The notes accurately locate proof mechanisms and do not confuse centrality of u with the stronger printed version.')
item(12,'reading question',[6,8],'124–127;181–196','H de présentation finie; comme H est séparé','The unit-homomorphism corollary repeats H where the preceding theorem needs finite presentation of G. In the kernel proof, closedness of ker(u) follows from separatedness of G, whereas the sentence says H.','Candidates G for the second H at line125 and G for H at line189 are proposed from the pullback ker(u)=H×(G,e)S.','agrees','The note correctly refuses to import an unrelated editorial H/G correction from Exposé X.')
item(13,'withdrawn in the same text',[6,8],'129–131;174–179;198–201','graphes des homomorphismes; Théorème5.1bis [deleted]','The subgroup version says multiplicative-type subgroups H1,H2 agreeing on a fiber agree near it when H1 is central and the ambient group satisfies the finiteness hypothesis. The headings and statement are struck on scan8.','The graph construction h↦(h,u(h)) identifies the homomorphism and subgroup versions. A local deletion is not evidence of mathematical falsity.','agrees','Both graph explanation and cancellation are present; print retains the subgroup theorem.')
item(14,'unproved claim',[18],'508–519','ouvert et fermé','For finite-type groups of multiplicative type, the character functor gives an anti-equivalence with twisted constant finitely generated abelian groups. Isom(H,G) is a clopen subfunctor of the representing Hom scheme; Aut(H) is twisted constant.','The character-group invertibility condition explains the clopen subfunctor. The entire reused leaf is struck, independently of its individual inserted et fermé.','agrees','The insertion is accurately recorded. The adjacent anti-equivalence, not separately noted, is included in the claims map.')
item(15,'reading question',[18,16,14],'521–540;446–474;366–379','du moins si S est quasi-compact','Isotriviality is compared with a union of clopen finite character-scheme pieces and finiteness of connected components. The proof constructs a finite étale splitting cover by choosing finitely many pieces of Isom(H,D(M)) whose images cover S.','The finite selection uses S quasi-compact or connected (with fixed type); the locally noetherian and non-finitely generated version has an independent published editorial reservation.','agrees','The note does not erase the inserted base restrictions or treat all three conditions as unconditionally equivalent.')
item(16,'withdrawn in the same text',[16],'435–444','de type fini [deleted]; quasi-compact [inserted]','A closed subscheme Z of a twisted constant S-scheme R is finite over S if Z→S is quasi-compact. After local trivialization and S affine, Z lies in a finite union of copies of S.','This is a strengthening of a finiteness hypothesis, not withdrawal of the result. Descent of a finite morphism is used.','agrees','The proof gives precisely the finite-subset argument the note describes.')
item(17,'unproved claim',[14,12],'381–397;284–287','Z_s soit fini; Alors Z est fini','For connected locally noetherian S, a clopen Z in a quasi-isotrivial twisted constant scheme P is finite if one fiber is finite. The proof makes the finite-fiber locus clopen and trivializes P étale locally.','No extension to a general non-noetherian base is inferred.','agrees','The note correctly identifies preservation of local noetherianity and the local-trivialization argument.')
item(18,'unproved claim',[14],'376–379','réunion d’une suite','A marginal criterion asserts isotriviality of a finite-type, constant-type multiplicative group when its character scheme is a countable union of clopen finite schemes, without restrictions on S.','The source does not construct one finite étale trivializing cover from the entire countable union. The published editorial question about the converse is retained.','agrees','The note marks the precise gap rather than transferring the earlier quasi-compact proof to this stronger claim.')
item('19,20','reading question',[12],'289–320','géométriquement unibranche au lieu de normal; Comme R est plat','The source extends finiteness of components and isotriviality from a locally noetherian normal base to a geometrically unibranch base using normalization of the reduction. It changes R to P inconsistently and ends with competing lemma references.','For the established finite-type theorem, the hypotheses are locally noetherian and geometrically unibranch; P in the component argument and the character or Isom scheme in the application must be distinguished.','agrees','The note accurately preserves the mixed letters. The generalization has a published destination; the residual lettering is a reading issue.')
item('21,22','reading question',[11,15],'241–275;402–410','fA-cofini; M/fM n’est pas fA-cofini','For a ring A with f regular and g regular modulo f, and A/(f,g)≠0, the draft constructs f-power-torsion M with successive annihilator quotients A/f and M/fM≅(A/(f,g))^(N). Under the modern Ext definition of I-cofinite, Ext^1_A(A/f,M)=M/fM is not finite.','The two-term free resolution of A/f computes Ext^0=ann_f M and Ext^1=M/fM. This tests terminology; it does not show that the draft used Hartshorne’s later definition.','agrees','The quoted historical word is not defined on the retained pages; the earlier-run Ext test is sound but cannot supply its historical meaning.')
item(23,'unpursued implication',[11,15,13,17],'267–275;402–429;324–359;478–501','Construction; Vérifions les propriétés; De plus','The logical order of the explicit module construction is scans11→15→13→17: specification, pushout, induction checks, direct limit.','This is an editorial dependency order; physical scan order and page numbers are retained.','agrees','All four transitions were checked directly against the named modules and equations.')
item('24,25','reading question',[13,15],'330–348;418–426','ηn≠0, donc; ξ̇n=(ġ,0)','The inference hηn=0⇒h∈fA requires ηn to correspond to a non-zero-divisor in B=A/f, not merely a nonzero element. The choice ξ̄n=(ḡ,0) with ḡ regular supplies the missing induction. In A=C[[x,y]], relations xe1=0, xej+ye(j−1)=0 give x^(n−1)(yen)=(-1)^(n−1)y^n e1 and annihilator (x).','The triangular presentation and regular-element induction are earlier-run reconstructions, checked here separately from the written implication.','agrees','The note correctly raises the missing regularity explanation. The uncertain equality/inequality sign at line348 is an ink question.')
item(26,'unproved claim',[17],'478–501','appliquant une base sur une base','For the pushout system En, Qn=En/fEn≅B⊕(B/g)^(n−1); the transition kills gQn and modulo g sends the old basis to part of the next basis. Consequently colim Qn≅(B/g)^(N).','A compatible presentation gives the inclusion of the first n coordinates in (B/g)^(n+1). The displayed n−1 in the direct limit is an inessential cofinal index shift, not a finite-rank equality.','agrees','The basis maps are asserted rather than printed as matrices; the note asks for exactly the needed calculation.')
item(27,'unproved claim',[20,22],'558–589;630–650','Conditions équivalentes; pt simple réel','For K finitely generated over Q, nonorderability, -1 a sum of squares, no embedding in R, absence of smooth real points on a model, and finite étale 2-cohomological dimension are compared. The real-field version and finite-type schemes over R/Z are also stated.','A model is an integral finite-type scheme with function field K. Smoothness/properness variants and their cancellations are kept distinct. Cohomological statements concern torsion étale sheaves.','agrees','The notes accurately report a list without proofs; they do not identify cancelled alternatives as live separate hypotheses.')
item('28,29','self-flagged step',[20],'558–566','suffit-il de degré de transcendance fini ??','Replacing finite generation by finite transcendence degree fails for the entire equivalence list: the real closure of Q(t) ordered with t>n for all integers n has transcendence degree one and is orderable, yet admits no field embedding into R.','The counterexample is the earlier run’s idea. Positivity in a real closed field is exactly nonzero squares; an embedding would preserve every square t−n and force its image larger than all n.','agrees','The explicit embedding obstruction answers the broad margin question; it does not decide every narrower cohomological variant.')
item(30,'idea of the earlier run',[20],'591–612','cd(X)<2dimX+1','The strict bound as transcribed fails for X=Spec F3: the separable irreducible polynomial z²+1 defines a nontrivial Z/2-torsor, so cd(X)≥1 while 2dimX+1=1.','The example is not in the manuscript. The plausible bound is ≤, but only the ink can determine whether the strict sign was written or misread.','agrees','Every hypothesis is satisfied: finite type over Z, dimension0, no real points, torsion étale coefficient Z/2. The counterexample targets the strict sign alone.')
item(31,'reading question',[22],'635–651','pour tt faisceau (étale) F; topologie ordinaire','When X is finite type over R with X(R)=∅, the draft compares étale cohomology with ordinary cohomology of X(C)/C2. A valid standard comparison requires torsion coefficients and analytification plus descent along the free conjugation action.','The quotient coefficient sheaf and torsion qualification are reconstruction; the unqualified all-sheaves statement is not supported by the standard comparison theorem.','agrees','The note properly flags the absent coefficient construction and qualification.')
item('32,33','reading question',[22],'653–682','cdr(k′)=cdr(k)+deg tr k′/k','The finite-type field-extension equality needs r≠char(k) and the real-field exception written in the source. Without the first restriction, k=Fp, k′=Fp(t), r=p gives cd_p=1 on both sides whereas the formula predicts2.','Nontrivial cyclic constant-field p-extensions and the Artin–Schreier upper bound justify the test. The missing prime restriction is a mathematical repair, not an editorial change.','agrees','The note’s counterexample satisfies finite generation and transcendence degree1 and isolates the omitted characteristic condition.')
item(34,'unproved claim',[23],'686–717','Corollaire; cdr(K)≥cdr(k)+dim A','For a noetherian local domain A, K=Frac(A), k its residue field, the draft asserts a residue-to-fraction-field lower bound with characteristic and real exceptions. The exact printed SGA4 X2.4 scope assumes r invertible in A and, for r=2, all residue fields nonorderable.','The source’s broader mixed-characteristic/regular clauses are separate from the printed comparator. Hua’s candidate-conjecture label is an earlier intake description, not the manuscript’s label.','agrees','The prior note already corrects the candidate assessment for the published scope; this run confirms that restriction.')
item(35,'self-flagged step',[23],'708–717','cette restriction est-elle inutile ???','The draft says catenary/universally Japanese hypotheses localize the real exception to the closed residue field, then says regularity removes that exception; a margin asks whether a restriction is unnecessary.','The precise antecedent of restriction remains unidentified. It may concern finiteness, regularity or the characteristic exception; no unique question is manufactured.','agrees','The note faithfully separates an unread antecedent from the asserted regular case.')
item(36,'withdrawn in the same text',[23],'686–693','hensélien [deleted]; avec égalité … ? [deleted]','The local lower-bound draft deletes henselianity from its assumptions and retracts its question proposing equality in the henselian case.','Modern equalities require precise excellence and prime hypotheses; the deletion is not a proof of failure under all henselian assumptions.','agrees','Both cancellations occur on the same page and are retained as drafting history.')
item(37,'idea of the earlier run',[23],'703–706','A=R[x,y]/(x²+y²) … localisé','At (x,y), A=R[x,y]/(x²+y²) is a one-dimensional local domain with residue R and fraction field C(t). The map x↦t,y↦it identifies the fraction field; cd2 R=∞ but cd2 C(t)=1.','The detailed kernel, fraction-field and cohomology computations are the earlier run’s expansion of his named example. A is singular, so it does not refute the regular-ring qualification.','agrees','The example exactly demonstrates why a real residue field can obstruct an unqualified lower bound.')
item(38,'unproved claim',[25,27],'730–773;812–863','il suffit … effacer … tt classe','For a site, abelian sheaf F, finite degree bound n and a class M of objects closed under the products actually needed for Čech intersections, the Cartan argument relates positive-degree vanishing, vanishing for every M-cover, and vanishing in the refinement colimit. Cofinality is weakened to effacement of relevant classes.','Closure under fiber products over S, not merely an unexamined absolute-product condition, is required for the stated proof.','agrees','The note identifies this exact closure issue and does not silently extend the finite-degree criterion to all degrees.')
item(39,'reading question',[27,31],'847–850;1020–1026','E_(2|4)^{pq}=0; p(≥|=)0','The refinement-limit spectral sequence has E2^{0,q}=0 for q>0 by local effacement. The earlier line has uncertain page index and p≥0 versus p=0; the later line explicitly says p=0.','Candidate E2 and p=0 are supported internally; the stronger all-column vanishing is not supplied by local effacement alone.','agrees','Both passages were compared; no diplomatic reading is replaced.')
